  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday morning through next Friday)\nIssued at 223 PM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThe large-scale mid-/upper-level pattern will be characterized by a \nridge to the west over the Plains, placing Georgia within northwest \nflow starting on Tuesday. This pattern can support mesoscale \nconvective systems (MCSs) along the northern periphery of the ridge \nthat dive southward across the Ohio/Tennessee River Valleys. If and \nwhere MCSs will develop and track (as well as where resulting \noutflow boundaries stall and spark convection the following day) \ngenerally have very low predictability more than 24 hours out, so \nPoPs and severe weather potential on Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday \nwill bear watching. FWIW, the AI-NWP-based forecasts and GEFS \nmachine learning forecasts from NCAR suggest that Marginal Risks \n(level 1 out of 5) may be needed on Monday and possibly Tuesday, \nwhich would not be surprising given the synoptic setup.\n\nEnsemble guidance signals a cold air damming (\"wedge\") setup across \nnorth and central Georgia Wednesday night through Friday night. This \nis reflected in our forecasted high temperatures (70s to 80s) and \nlow temperatures (mid-50s to mid-60s). While still seasonable \ntemperatures for this time of year, it will be noticeably cooler \nthan conditions this weekend. Additionally, moisture will decrease \nduring this time, as the drier wedge airmass and northerly low-/mid-\nlevel winds prevail. Still, enough moisture will remain to support a \nslight chance for showers on Thursday and Friday, although the vast \nmajority of the forecast area looks to stay dry. It may not be true \nautumnal weather, but given that the first day of astronomical fall \nis Tuesday, I will take it a slight cooldown!",
